04. Revisionist is aggrieved with the said order and has assailed the impugned order on various grounds which can be summarized as under :-
i) That the impugned is against the settled principle of law and as such same is bad in law as well as based on conjectures and surmises;
ii) That the impugned order is illegal, perverse and without application of mind;
(iii) That Ld.Trial Court failed to appreciate that averment of the complaint disclosed the commission of cognizable offence punishable u/s 406 and 420 IPC;
(iv) That Ld.Trial Court failed to consider that the revisionist has paid money at the time of agreement to sell to the father of the respondent no.1 and 2 and this agreement was orally approved of by the respondents;
(v) That Ld. Trial Court failed to appreciate the fact that despite part payment of sale consideration, the respondent has neither got registered the sale deed nor returned back the money;
(vi) That Ld. Trial Court failed to consider that in the attending facts and circumstances police investigation is required to collect all the evidence, which is not in control of the revisionist.
Crl Rev. No. 505/2024 Ravi Kumar Solanki v. Bharat Kapur And Anrs.
